Read `AGENTS.md` and `docs/INVARIANTS.md` before writing code. They are the
contract; this file is the short version of it.

## Rhythm

I build iteratively. Scaffold, play, revise, settle. Your job is to keep up with
that rhythm rather than to impose a plan on it.

- Make the smallest change that lets me see the thing working.
- Do not write planning documents, roadmaps, or phase breakdowns unless asked.
- Do not refactor code outside the change I requested. Note it in
  `docs/REPAIR-QUEUE.md` instead.
- Do not add abstraction until three real callers exist.
- Do not write tests during a spike. We backfill with `/backfill` once the shape
  stops moving.
- Keep diffs small. More than roughly six files touched means stop and check in.

## Where work happens

Day-to-day work happens on a long-lived `play` branch, never directly on
`main`. Settled batches reach `main` through one pull request, merged with a
merge commit. If you find yourself on `main` with changes to make, say so
before committing; the boundary check rejects any spike marker there.

The loop has commands: `/status`, `/play`, `/ship`, `/land`, plus `/sync`
(harness updates), `/doctor` (harness health), `/debt` (the ratchet's long
view), `/authtest` (tests proving each endpoint turns away the wrong caller),
`/offline` (tests proving offline work arrives exactly once), `/perf`
(Speed Insights, a bundle budget, slow queries) and `/monitor` (Sentry error
monitoring). When I ask where things stand, to open, merge, or move work
between branches, to update or check the harness, about debt, or to test
authorization or offline behaviour, measure performance, or set up error
monitoring, follow the matching command in `.cursor/commands/` rather than
improvising.

## Marking rough work

A spike is not a branch, a mode, or a phase. It is a label on a specific piece
of code that is knowingly not fit to ship. Most experimenting needs no marker:
a new layout that works is just code, even if it is gone tomorrow.

The test: if this could go to production exactly as written, no marker. If it
could not, mark it. That means corners cut on purpose, such as faked or skipped
auth, hardcoded data standing in for a query, a missing error state, a disabled
check. Put a dated marker at the top of the file or block with one line saying
what is unfinished:

```ts
// SPIKE(2026-09-22): hardcoded race list until the query shape settles
```

Use today's date. Whenever you cut such a corner, add the marker yourself
without being asked. CI rejects spike markers on any pull request into the
default branch, and the harness starts nagging about a marker after thirty
days, so this is how rough code stays honest instead of quietly becoming
permanent.

## Things the ratchet counts

The harness keeps a per-file count of these and will not let the total rise on
the way to main. Avoid them by default; when one is genuinely warranted, that is
fine, but know it is being counted: `any` and `as any`, `@ts-ignore`,
`eslint-disable` (if you must, add a reason after `--`), non-null assertions
(`foo!.bar`), and `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.

## Check your own work

Before you tell me a change is done, run `npx harness quick` and fix whatever it
reports. It takes a few seconds. Finding it yourself is better than me finding it
at commit time, and far better than CI finding it.

## Tell me, do not decide for me

Pause and ask before: adding a dependency, changing a schema, touching an auth
path, adding a background job or cron, sending data to a third party, or adding
an environment variable.

## Style

Write in the style already present in the file you are editing. Match its naming,
its error handling, its import order. Consistency with the surrounding code beats
your preferred idiom every time.
